.TH AM 1 "November 2025" "2.1.2" "Alias Manager Manual"
.SH NAME
am \- manage shell aliases efficiently
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B am
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B am
is a lightweight command-line tool for managing shell aliases. It provides a simple interface to add, remove, and list aliases stored in
.IR ~/.config/am/aliases.txt .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "init " [\fIshell\fR]
Interactive setup that adds alias loading to your shell configuration file.
Auto-detects the shell from $SHELL if not specified.
Use
.B "init --hook"
to output only the shell code for manual integration.
Supported shells: bash, zsh, fish.
.TP
.BR "add " \fIname\fR " " \fIcommand\fR...
Create or update an alias. If an alias with the same name exists, it will be replaced.
.TP
.BR "rm " \fIname\fR " " [\fI-f\fR]
Remove an alias. Without
.IR -f ,
prompts for confirmation.
.TP
.BR "ls " [\fIpattern\fR]
List all aliases, or filter by pattern if provided.
The pattern matches both alias names and commands.
.TP
.BR "reload " [\fIshell\fR]
Output the command to reload aliases in the current shell.
Use with
.BR "eval $(am reload)" .
.TP
.B help
Show help message.
.TP
.B version
Show version information.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help message (same as
.BR "help" ).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information (same as
.BR "version" ).
.TP
.B \-f
Force operation without confirmation (for
.BR rm " command)."
.SH FILES
The alias file location is determined by the following priority:
.TP
.I $AM_ALIAS_FILE
Custom alias file path (highest priority). Useful for testing or custom setups.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/am/aliases.txt
XDG standard configuration location if XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set.
.TP
.I ~/.config/am/aliases.txt
Default XDG configuration location.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AM_ALIAS_FILE
Override the alias file location. Useful for testing or custom setups.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Standard XDG configuration directory. If set, aliases are stored in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/am/aliases.txt .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Interactive setup (run once):
.B am init
.TP
Create an alias for git log:
.B am add gl git log \-\-oneline \-\-graph
.TP
List all aliases:
.B am ls
.TP
Search for git-related aliases:
.B am ls git
.TP
Reload aliases in current shell:
.B eval "$(am reload)"
.TP
Remove an alias with confirmation:
.B am rm gl
.TP
Remove an alias without confirmation:
.B am rm gl \-f
.TP
Manual shell integration (alternative to interactive setup):
.B eval "$(am init --hook)" >> ~/.bashrc
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments
.TP
.B 2
File access error
.TP
.B 3
Home directory not found
.TP
.B 4
System error
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
The recommended way to integrate with your shell is to use the interactive init command:
.PP
.RS
.B am init
.RE
.PP
This will automatically detect your shell and add the necessary configuration to your shell's RC file.
.PP
For manual integration, you can use the --hook flag:
.PP
.RS
.B Bash:
eval "$(am init --hook)" in ~/.bashrc
.br
.B Zsh:
eval "$(am init --hook)" in ~/.zshrc
.br
.B Fish:
am init --hook fish | source in ~/.config/fish/config.fish
.RE
